<!-- Saint Simon the Zealot
Simon (Σίμων) the Zealot/Cananaean: A Galilean Disciple

(Parallel to "Simon bar Jonah: A Galilean Fisherman")

Name, Identity, and Social Position
Shares the name "Simon" with Simon Peter — the second of two apostles named Simon among the Twelve, requiring the same kind of immediate disambiguation given to Bartholomew/Nathanael and the two Jameses
Distinguished in the apostolic lists by an added title: "the Cananaean" (Matthew 10:4; Mark 3:18) and "the Zealot" (Luke 6:15; Acts 1:13)
"Cananaean" is not a reference to Canaan or to the town of Cana, but a transliteration of the Aramaic "qanai," meaning "zealous one" — so "Cananaean" and "Zealot" are two translations of the same underlying description rather than two separate facts about him, a point worth stating clearly since the two titles look geographically confusing at first glance
The title likely indicates either membership in, or sympathy with, the Zealot movement — a Jewish faction associated with militant resistance to Roman occupation, though the formal Zealot party as a organized political-military movement is more firmly attested in the years leading up to the Jewish revolt of AD 66-70, somewhat later than Jesus's ministry — worth flagging as a genuine historical question rather than asserting he was a card-carrying member of a fully formed party
Alternatively, "zealous" could describe a personal disposition of intense religious fervor rather than formal political affiliation, similar to how Paul later describes his own pre-conversion "zeal" for the Law (Philippians 3:6) without that being a formal party title
No hometown, trade, or additional biographical marker preserved in Scripture beyond this title, continuing the pattern of sparse biographical detail seen with Thomas, James son of Alphaeus, and Jude Thaddaeus
Family Structure & Responsibilities
No family details preserved in Scripture — no named parents, siblings, or trade, matching the total silence already noted for James son of Alphaeus
Social and Cultural Environment
If the Zealot identification is political rather than merely temperamental, Simon would represent the sharpest possible contrast within the Twelve: a former or sympathetic revolutionary called into the same band as Matthew, a former agent of the very Roman tax system Zealots resisted
This pairing is worth stating explicitly as one of the most striking juxtapositions among the Twelve — a tax collector and a zealot, natural political enemies, both called by the same Jesus into the same apostolic band, with no recorded conflict between them in the text
Religious Life
No individual statement, question, or action attributed to him anywhere in the four Gospels — placing him alongside James son of Alphaeus as one of the two least individually attested apostles in the canonical record
His title itself is the only window Scripture gives into his religious or political disposition prior to his calling
Political Awareness
By virtue of his title alone, Simon carries the most explicit political identity marker of any apostle in this series — where Matthew's tax-collecting implies collaboration with Rome, Simon's "Zealot" designation implies (at least sympathetically) opposition to it
Worth noting that Jesus's calling of both men into the same inner circle, without either renouncing their past position on the record, has long been read by commentators as a deliberate statement about the kingdom of God transcending contemporary political factionalism
Reputation in the Community
Entirely undocumented outside his title and his consistent inclusion in the Twelve
Alongside James son of Alphaeus, the least individually narrated member of the apostolic band in the Gospels and Acts alike
Simon the Called

(Parallel to "The Day the Nets Were Full" and the calling sections of the other apostles)

No individual calling narrative recorded in Scripture, matching the gap already noted for Thomas, James son of Alphaeus, and Jude Thaddaeus
First appears simply as a name within the formal appointment of the Twelve (Mark 3:13-19; Luke 6:12-16), consistently distinguished from Simon Peter by his added title in every list
Continues and now firmly establishes the pattern: a majority of the Twelve enter the narrative purely through the list itself, with no personal calling story attached — worth noting that by this point in your series, the "calling narrative" apostles (Peter, Andrew, James/John Zebedee, Philip, Matthew) are actually the exception rather than the rule among the Twelve
Simon's Journey

(Parallel to "Cephas's Journey": Walking on Water, Transfiguration, Denial/Restoration)

A Complete Absence of Individual Narrative
No episode, question, statement, or action is attributed to Simon the Zealot individually anywhere in the four Gospels
His entire scriptural presence consists of his name and title appearing in the four lists of the Twelve (Matthew 10:4; Mark 3:18; Luke 6:15; Acts 1:13)
As with James son of Alphaeus, this should be stated directly rather than padded — there is no episode to reconstruct here, only the fact of his title and his consistent inclusion among the Twelve
The one substantive point of interest remains structural rather than narrative: his presence beside Matthew in the same apostolic band, discussed above
Simon the Apostle of the Early Church

(Parallel to Peter's leadership: Pentecost, Miracles, Imprisonment, Council)

Holy Spirit Praxis
Listed among the Twelve gathered in the upper room after the Ascension (Acts 1:13), named there as "Simon the Zealot"
Present at Pentecost by inclusion in "the Twelve," though no individual action is attributed to him anywhere in Acts, continuing the pattern of Philip, Bartholomew, Thomas, Matthew, James son of Alphaeus, and Jude Thaddaeus
Absence from Individual Narrative in Acts
Simon recedes from the biblical narrative entirely after Acts 1:13, making this the seventh consecutive apostle in your series with this exact shape
Like James son of Alphaeus, he leaves behind no writing of any kind attributed to him under his own name
The Council of Jerusalem
No individual role attributed to him in Acts 15
Presumed presence among "the apostles and elders" as a member of the Twelve, though not named specifically
Simon's Later Ministry

(A distinctive tradition-dependent section, directly continuous with Jude Thaddaeus's "Later Ministry" section)

As established on Jude's page, strong and long-standing tradition pairs Simon with Jude Thaddaeus for a joint missionary ministry, particularly in Persia and Mesopotamia
Some traditions place earlier, separate ministry activity for Simon in Egypt, North Africa, or even Britain (a distinctly Western legendary strand with weak historical support), before his later joining with Jude in the Persian mission field
The Egyptian/North African tradition is sometimes linked to a separate strand naming him bishop of Jerusalem in some confused later sources — likely a conflation with Simeon, a later leader of the Jerusalem church and relative of Jesus, distinct from Simon the Zealot, worth flagging explicitly as its own disambiguation problem rather than folding the two figures together
As with all later-ministry sections in this series, these accounts should be presented plainly as tradition of uneven reliability, with the Jude pairing in Persia being the most consistently attested strand, exactly as noted on Jude's page
Simon's Writings

(Parallel to "James son of Alphaeus's Writings" — his closest structural match in the series)

No writing of any kind is attributed to Simon the Zealot, canonical or apocryphal
This places him alongside James son of Alphaeus as the only two members of the Twelve with no literary legacy whatsoever — worth stating as a direct, explicit parallel between the two pages
No apocryphal "Gospel" or "Acts" bearing his name individually survives, unlike the apocryphal literature attached to Thomas, Philip, or Bartholomew; what apocryphal material exists (such as a Passion of Simon and Jude) treats him jointly with Jude rather than as an individual subject
The Final Journey

(Parallel to the Final Journey sections of all preceding apostles)

What the Bible Tells Us
Scripture gives no account whatsoever of his later life, ministry, or death — his last scriptural appearance is his listing in Acts 1:13, a silence as total as James son of Alphaeus's, though here at least a title (Zealot/Cananaean) is preserved where James has none
Simon's Ministry and Death in Persia (Early Christian Tradition)
Tradition holds he was martyred in Persia alongside Jude Thaddaeus, continuing their consistently joint missionary and martyrdom tradition
Accounts vary on the specific manner of death: some traditions describe crucifixion, others describe being sawn in half — the latter becoming his standard iconographic attribute in Christian art, paralleling how Jude's axe, Bartholomew's flaying knife, and Thomas's spear each became fixed visual identifiers
Relics traditionally associated with Simon are held at St. Peter's Basilica in Rome, alongside those attributed to Jude, reinforcing their shared devotional treatment even in the modern era
Simon's Martyrdom
The Persia tradition, shared jointly with Jude, carries the same relative consistency across Eastern and Western sources already noted on Jude's page, though the "sawn in half" detail is somewhat later and more legendary than the core fact of martyrdom in Persia itself
Simon's Legacy

(Parallel to "Jude's Legacy" / "James son of Alphaeus's Legacy")

Patron saint of tanners and curriers in some traditions (an association shared with Bartholomew, likely owing to the sawing/cutting imagery associated with both men's martyrdoms), and of woodworkers or sawyers in others, again tied directly to the manner of his traditional death
Feast day observed jointly with Jude Thaddaeus in Western tradition (October 28), making theirs the only fully shared feast day among the Twelve in this series
His legacy, like James son of Alphaeus's, rests almost entirely on structural significance rather than personal narrative: proof that the apostolic band deliberately included men of sharply opposed political backgrounds (a zealot and a tax collector) without recorded friction, a detail with real theological weight despite the absence of any individual episode to dramatize it
Represents, alongside James son of Alphaeus, the clearest embodiment of a theme worth naming explicitly by this point in your series: apostolic calling and authority in the New Testament are not contingent on individual prominence, eloquence, writing, or a developed personal story
The joint Simon-Jude tradition, from missionary pairing through martyrdom through shared feast day through shared relics, makes their two pages the most thoroughly interlinked entries in your entire set -->